export const seedCodeSections: CodeSection[] = [
  {
    id: 'cs-070',
    section_number: '070',
    title: 'Plumbing Fixtures',
    summary:
      'This section covers the requirements for plumbing fixtures installed in buildings throughout Kentucky. It establishes the minimum number and types of fixtures required based on building occupancy and use, and it sets standards for fixture quality, installation, and accessibility. Every plumbing fixture must be made of approved materials with smooth, impervious surfaces that are easy to clean.\n\nWater closets must be connected to a soil pipe no smaller than 3 inches in diameter and must be equipped with a flushing device capable of adequately clearing the bowl in a single flush. Lavatories, sinks, bathtubs, and showers must be properly trapped and vented according to the code. Floor drains are required in mechanical rooms, commercial kitchens, public restrooms, and other areas where water accumulation is likely.\n\nFixtures must be installed with adequate clearance for use and maintenance. Water closets require a minimum of 15 inches from the centerline to any side wall or obstruction, and at least 21 inches of clear space in front. Fixtures must be set level and secured to their supports. All fixture connections to the drainage system must be made with approved fittings, and no fixture outlet shall be smaller than the size specified in the drainage tables.',
    full_text:
      'PLUMBING FIXTURES (815 KAR 20:070)\n\nMinimum Fixture Requirements:\n- Every building intended for human occupancy must have adequate plumbing fixtures.\n- Minimum fixtures include: water closet, lavatory, and a source of drinking water.\n- Commercial and public buildings require additional fixtures based on occupancy load.\n\nWater Closets:\n- Must connect to a soil pipe of not less than 3 inches in diameter.\n- Must be equipped with an approved flushing mechanism.\n- Floor-mounted water closets must be set on an approved flange securely bolted to the floor.\n- Wall-hung water closets must be rigidly supported by an approved carrier fitting.\n- Minimum clearance: 15 inches from centerline to nearest side wall or obstruction.\n- Minimum clearance: 21 inches in front of the water closet to any wall, door, or fixture.\n\nLavatories:\n- Must be made of approved materials (vitreous china, enameled cast iron, stainless steel, or other approved material).\n- Must be equipped with an approved waste fitting.\n- Overflow must connect to the inlet side of the trap.\n\nBathtubs and Showers:\n- Bathtubs must have a minimum waste outlet of 1-1/2 inches.\n- Shower drains must be at least 2 inches in diameter.\n- Shower compartments must have a minimum finished interior of 900 square inches with a minimum dimension of 30 inches.\n- Shower walls must be finished with a smooth, non-absorbent, waterproof material to a height of not less than 6 feet.\n\nSinks:\n- Kitchen sinks must have a minimum waste outlet of 1-1/2 inches.\n- Service sinks and mop basins must have a minimum waste outlet of 2 inches (for 3-inch trap) or as specified.\n- Sinks used for food preparation must have an indirect waste connection where required by health code.\n\nDrinking Fountains:\n- Must be of an approved sanitary type.\n- Must not be installed in restrooms.\n- Required in public buildings, schools, and commercial buildings.\n\nFloor Drains:\n- Required in restrooms of public and commercial buildings.\n- Required in mechanical equipment rooms.\n- Must have a minimum 2-inch drain.\n- Must be provided with an accessible trap primer or other approved method to maintain the trap seal.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 1,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-080',
    section_number: '080',
    title: 'Waste Pipe Size',
    summary:
      'This section establishes the sizing requirements for waste pipes, building drains, and building sewers in the plumbing system. All drain pipe sizing is based on Drainage Fixture Units (DFUs), which represent the probable discharge rate from various plumbing fixtures. Each fixture type is assigned a specific DFU value, and the total DFU load determines the minimum pipe size for each segment of the drainage system.\n\nThe section includes critical slope requirements for horizontal drainage piping. Pipes 2-1/2 inches and smaller must be installed with a minimum slope of 1/4 inch per foot, while pipes 3 inches through 6 inches require a minimum slope of 1/8 inch per foot. These slopes ensure proper drainage by gravity and prevent solids from settling in the pipe. Building drains and sewers must be sized to handle the total DFU load of all fixtures connected to them.\n\nThe DFU values and maximum loading tables are essential for exam preparation. Undersizing drainage piping is a code violation that can lead to stoppages and backups, while oversizing wastes materials and can reduce flow velocity below the self-scouring threshold. The code also addresses the sizing of horizontal and vertical waste stacks, branch intervals, and building drains that receive discharge from multiple stories.',
    full_text:
      'WASTE PIPE SIZE (815 KAR 20:080)\n\nDrainage Fixture Unit (DFU) Values:\n- Lavatory: 1 DFU\n- Drinking fountain: 1/2 DFU\n- Bathtub (with or without shower): 2 DFU\n- Shower stall: 2 DFU\n- Water closet (1.6 GPF or less): 3 DFU\n- Water closet (greater than 1.6 GPF): 4 DFU\n- Kitchen sink (residential): 2 DFU\n- Kitchen sink (commercial with grease trap): 4 DFU\n- Laundry tub / clothes washer standpipe: 2 DFU\n- Floor drain (2-inch): 2 DFU\n- Floor drain (3-inch): 3 DFU\n- Service sink / mop basin: 3 DFU\n- Urinal (flush valve): 4 DFU\n- Urinal (flush tank): 2 DFU\n\nMinimum Trap and Waste Pipe Sizes:\n- Lavatory: 1-1/4 inch\n- Bathtub: 1-1/2 inch\n- Shower: 2 inch\n- Water closet: 3 inch (minimum soil pipe)\n- Kitchen sink: 1-1/2 inch\n- Laundry: 1-1/2 inch (2-inch standpipe)\n- Floor drain: 2 inch minimum\n\nMaximum DFU Loading by Pipe Size (Horizontal Branch):\n- 1-1/2 inch: 3 DFU\n- 2 inch: 6 DFU\n- 2-1/2 inch: 12 DFU\n- 3 inch: 20 DFU\n- 4 inch: 160 DFU\n- 6 inch: 620 DFU\n\nMaximum DFU Loading by Pipe Size (Building Drain / Sewer):\n- 3 inch: 36 DFU (at 1/8" per foot slope)\n- 4 inch: 216 DFU (at 1/8" per foot slope)\n- 6 inch: 720 DFU (at 1/8" per foot slope)\n- 8 inch: 2,640 DFU (at 1/8" per foot slope)\n\nSlope Requirements for Horizontal Drainage Piping:\n- Pipes 2-1/2 inches and smaller: minimum 1/4 inch per foot fall.\n- Pipes 3 inches through 6 inches: minimum 1/8 inch per foot fall.\n- Pipes 8 inches and larger: minimum 1/16 inch per foot fall.\n\nVertical Waste Stack Capacity:\n- 1-1/2 inch stack: 2 DFU per branch interval, 4 DFU total\n- 2 inch stack: 6 DFU per branch interval, 10 DFU total\n- 3 inch stack: 20 DFU per branch interval, 48 DFU total (30 for more than 3 branch intervals)\n- 4 inch stack: 90 DFU per branch interval, 240 DFU total\n- 6 inch stack: 350 DFU per branch interval, 960 DFU total\n\nGeneral Rules:\n- No horizontal drainage pipe shall be reduced in size in the direction of flow.\n- The minimum size of any building drain or building sewer is 3 inches.\n- A 3-inch building drain receiving the discharge of one or more water closets may serve a maximum of 2 water closets.\n- A 4-inch building drain is required for 3 or more water closets.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 2,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-090',
    section_number: '090',
    title: 'Soil, Waste, Vent Systems, Traps, and Clean-Outs',
    summary:
      'This section is one of the most critical parts of the Kentucky Plumbing Code and differs significantly from many other state codes. Kentucky does NOT allow wet venting. Every plumbing fixture must be individually vented with its own dry vent pipe that connects to the vent stack or stack vent. This is a fundamental requirement that is frequently tested on the Kentucky plumbing exam. The vent system serves to protect trap seals from siphonage and back-pressure, and to provide air circulation within the drainage system.\n\nTraps are required on every fixture and must maintain a water seal of not less than 2 inches and not more than 4 inches deep. Each trap must be self-cleaning and must be installed as close to the fixture outlet as possible. The maximum allowable distance from the fixture outlet to the trap weir (trap arm length) varies by pipe size: 1-1/4 inch pipe allows 2-1/2 feet, 1-1/2 inch pipe allows 3-1/2 feet (or 42 inches), 2 inch pipe allows 5 feet, and 3 inch pipe allows 6 feet. Double trapping is prohibited. S-traps, drum traps (except where specifically permitted), and crown-vented traps are not allowed.\n\nCleanouts are required at the upper end of every horizontal drainage run, at every change of direction greater than 45 degrees, and at intervals not exceeding 100 feet for pipes 4 inches and larger, and 50 feet for pipes smaller than 4 inches. Cleanouts must be the same nominal size as the pipe they serve up to 4 inches, and not less than 4 inches for larger pipes. The vent stack must extend full size through the roof and terminate at least 6 inches above the roof surface, or 12 inches where the roof is used for purposes other than weather protection. Vent terminals must be at least 10 feet from any openable window, door, or air intake.',
    full_text:
      'SOIL, WASTE, VENT SYSTEMS, TRAPS, AND CLEAN-OUTS (815 KAR 20:090)\n\nVENTING REQUIREMENTS:\n\nCRITICAL: Kentucky does NOT permit wet venting. Every fixture must be individually vented.\n- Each plumbing fixture must have an individual vent.\n- Wet venting, circuit venting, and loop venting are NOT allowed in Kentucky.\n- Common venting is permitted only for back-to-back fixtures on the same floor level sharing the same drain.\n- Stack venting is permitted for a single-story building with one bathroom group (water closet, lavatory, and bathtub/shower) where the stack is no more than 4 DFU.\n- The vent stack must extend full size through the roof.\n\nVent Pipe Sizing:\n- Minimum individual vent size: 1-1/4 inch (for fixtures with 1-1/4 or 1-1/2 inch drains).\n- Minimum vent for a 2-inch drain: 1-1/2 inch.\n- Minimum vent for a 3-inch drain (water closet): 2 inch.\n- Minimum vent for a 4-inch drain: 3 inch.\n- Vent stack size must be at least one-half the diameter of the soil/waste stack it serves, but not less than 1-1/4 inch.\n\nVent Terminal Requirements:\n- Vent pipes must extend undiminished in size through the roof.\n- Vent terminal must be at least 6 inches above the roof surface.\n- Where a roof is used for assembly or other purposes: 12 inches minimum above roof.\n- Vent terminal must be at least 10 feet horizontally from any openable window, door, or air intake.\n- Where vent terminal is within 10 feet of a vertical surface that extends above the vent, the vent must extend at least 2 feet above the surface.\n- In areas subject to frost or snow, vent extension through the roof must be at least 2 inches in diameter.\n\nTRAP REQUIREMENTS:\n- Every fixture must have a trap.\n- Trap seal depth: minimum 2 inches, maximum 4 inches of water.\n- Traps must be self-cleaning.\n- Traps must be installed as close to the fixture outlet as possible.\n- Double trapping is prohibited (no more than one trap per fixture).\n- S-traps are prohibited.\n- Bell traps are prohibited.\n- Crown-vented traps are prohibited.\n- Drum traps: generally prohibited except for special applications (floor drains, specific retrofits).\n- Building (house) trap: NOT required (prohibited except where specifically mandated by the local authority).\n\nMaximum Trap Arm Distances (from fixture outlet to vent):\n- 1-1/4 inch pipe: 2 feet 6 inches (30 inches)\n- 1-1/2 inch pipe: 3 feet 6 inches (42 inches)\n- 2 inch pipe: 5 feet (60 inches)\n- 3 inch pipe: 6 feet (72 inches)\n- 4 inch pipe: 10 feet (120 inches)\n\nCLEAN-OUT REQUIREMENTS:\n- Required at the upper terminal of every horizontal drainage run.\n- Required at each change of direction exceeding 45 degrees in the building drain or horizontal branch.\n- Required at intervals of not more than 100 feet for 4-inch and larger pipes.\n- Required at intervals of not more than 50 feet for pipes smaller than 4 inches.\n- Cleanout size: same nominal size as the pipe, up to 4 inches. For pipes larger than 4 inches, the cleanout must be at least 4 inches.\n- Cleanouts must be accessible and provide adequate clearance for cable or rodding equipment.\n- Cleanout plugs or caps must be brass or approved plastic.\n- A cleanout or accessible fitting must be installed within 5 feet of the building wall where the building drain exits.\n\nGENERAL DRAINAGE RULES:\n- All soil and waste piping must be installed with uniform grade (slope).\n- No fitting that restricts flow (such as a tee turned on its side) shall be used in the drainage system.\n- Horizontal drainage pipe must use sanitary tees, wyes, combination wye-and-1/8 bends, or long-sweep quarter bends.\n- Vertical to horizontal connections must use appropriate fittings.\n- Changes in direction of drainage piping must be made with approved fittings.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 3,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-120',
    section_number: '120',
    title: 'Water Supply and Distribution',
    summary:
      'This section governs the design, installation, and maintenance of water supply and distribution systems in buildings. It covers everything from the water service pipe entering the building through the individual fixture supply connections. The minimum size for a water service pipe to a building is 3/4 inch, and the pipe must be sized to provide adequate flow and pressure to all fixtures under peak demand conditions.\n\nWater pressure in the distribution system must not exceed 80 PSI. Where street pressure exceeds 80 PSI, an approved pressure-reducing valve must be installed. Conversely, where pressure is insufficient to serve upper-floor fixtures, a booster pump system may be required. The water distribution system must be designed to deliver minimum flow rates and pressures at each fixture: typically 8 PSI at the fixture for most outlets. Hot water must be available at all fixtures requiring it, and the system must limit hot water temperature at public handwashing lavatories to 110 degrees Fahrenheit maximum.\n\nBackflow prevention is a major component of this section. Every potable water system must be protected from contamination through cross-connections. Backflow preventers must be installed based on the degree of hazard: atmospheric vacuum breakers, pressure vacuum breakers, double check valve assemblies, and reduced pressure zone (RPZ) assemblies are specified for different applications. An air gap (a physical separation of at least two pipe diameters, but not less than 1 inch) is the most reliable form of backflow prevention. All backflow prevention assemblies must be testable and must be tested upon installation and annually thereafter.',
    full_text:
      'WATER SUPPLY AND DISTRIBUTION (815 KAR 20:120)\n\nWater Service Pipe:\n- Minimum size of water service pipe: 3/4 inch.\n- Water service pipe must be sized based on available pressure, developed length, fixture units, and friction loss.\n- Water service pipe material must be approved (copper, CPVC, PEX, ductile iron, or other approved materials).\n- Water service pipe must have a minimum cover of 12 inches below the frost line (typically 18-24 inches total in Kentucky).\n- A main shutoff valve must be installed at or near the point of entry into the building and must be accessible.\n\nWater Pressure:\n- Maximum allowable water pressure: 80 PSI.\n- Where street main pressure exceeds 80 PSI, an approved pressure-reducing valve (PRV) is required.\n- Minimum pressure at any fixture: 8 PSI (15 PSI for flush valve fixtures).\n- Pressure-reducing valves must be accessible for maintenance and repair.\n- Thermal expansion must be addressed when a PRV or backflow preventer creates a closed system. An approved expansion tank or thermal expansion device is required.\n\nWater Distribution Pipe Sizing:\n- Based on Water Supply Fixture Unit (WSFU) values.\n- Minimum pipe sizes to fixtures:\n  - Lavatory: 3/8 inch\n  - Water closet (tank type): 3/8 inch\n  - Water closet (flush valve): 1 inch\n  - Bathtub: 1/2 inch\n  - Shower: 1/2 inch\n  - Kitchen sink: 1/2 inch\n  - Laundry: 1/2 inch\n  - Hose bibb: 1/2 inch\n  - Dishwasher: 1/2 inch\n\nHot Water Systems:\n- Every building with plumbing fixtures requiring hot water must have a hot water supply.\n- Water heaters must be installed in accordance with manufacturer instructions and applicable codes.\n- Maximum hot water temperature at public handwashing lavatories: 110°F.\n- Temperature and pressure (T&P) relief valve required on all storage-type water heaters.\n- T&P relief valve discharge pipe must terminate within 6 inches of the floor or to an approved indirect waste receptor.\n- T&P discharge pipe must not be reduced in size, must not have any valve installed, and must not be threaded at the terminal end.\n- Hot water recirculation systems must have a check valve and must not create cross-connections.\n\nBackflow Prevention:\n- All potable water systems must be protected against backflow and cross-connections.\n- Air Gap: minimum physical separation of 2 pipe diameters (but not less than 1 inch) between the supply outlet and the flood level rim.\n- Atmospheric Vacuum Breaker (AVB): for non-continuous pressure applications without back-pressure (hose bibbs, irrigation).\n- Pressure Vacuum Breaker (PVB): for continuous pressure applications without back-pressure.\n- Double Check Valve Assembly (DCVA): for low-hazard cross-connections with back-pressure potential.\n- Reduced Pressure Zone (RPZ) Assembly: for high-hazard cross-connections. Required for connections to boilers, fire sprinkler systems with chemical additives, and other health-hazard applications.\n- Backflow assemblies must be tested upon installation and at least annually by a certified tester.\n- No direct connection between potable and non-potable water systems.\n- Lawn irrigation systems require backflow prevention (minimum PVB or RPZ depending on chemical injection).\n\nWater Hammer:\n- Water hammer arresters must be installed where quick-closing valves are used.\n- Arresters must comply with ASSE 1010 and be installed as close to the quick-closing valve as possible.\n\nValves:\n- Individual shutoff valves required at each fixture supply.\n- A main shutoff valve is required on the building water service.\n- Shutoff valves required on both hot and cold water supply to each water heater.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 4,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-130',
    section_number: '130',
    title: 'House Sewers and Storm Water Piping',
    summary:
      'This section addresses the installation requirements for building sewers (also called house sewers) that connect the building drain to the public sewer or private disposal system, as well as storm water drainage piping. The building sewer begins 5 feet outside the building foundation wall or at the point designated by the authority having jurisdiction. Building sewers must be sized to handle the total drainage fixture unit load from the building.\n\nStorm water drainage must be kept separate from the sanitary sewer unless a combined sewer system is specifically approved by the local jurisdiction. Where separate storm drainage is required, roof drains, area drains, and yard drains must connect to a dedicated storm sewer or discharge to an approved point. Storm drain piping must be sized based on the maximum rainfall rate (inches per hour) and the projected roof or surface area being drained. Combined sewers, where they are permitted, must be sized for both sanitary and storm loads.\n\nBuilding sewers must be installed with proper slope, bedding, and backfill to prevent settling and damage. The minimum slope is 1/8 inch per foot for 4-inch and larger pipe, and 1/4 inch per foot for smaller pipe. Cleanouts are required at the upper end, at every change of direction exceeding 45 degrees, and at intervals not exceeding 100 feet. Sewer pipe materials include PVC, cast iron, ductile iron, ABS, and vitrified clay, and must be approved for below-grade installation.',
    full_text:
      'HOUSE SEWERS AND STORM WATER PIPING (815 KAR 20:130)\n\nBuilding (House) Sewer:\n- The building sewer begins 5 feet outside the building foundation wall (or as designated by the authority having jurisdiction).\n- Minimum size of building sewer: 4 inches for residential, sized by DFU tables for commercial.\n- Minimum slope: 1/4 inch per foot for pipes smaller than 4 inches; 1/8 inch per foot for 4-inch and larger pipes.\n- Depth of cover must be adequate to prevent freezing (below frost line).\n- Building sewer must be installed on a firm bed of approved material, with uniform support.\n- Backfill must be free of large rocks, debris, or frozen materials.\n\nSewer Materials:\n- Approved materials for building sewers include: PVC (SDR 35 or Schedule 40), ABS, cast iron, ductile iron, vitrified clay, and concrete pipe (for storm only).\n- All joints must be watertight.\n- Pipe laid in filled or unstable ground must be of approved strength or must be supported on an approved foundation.\n\nSewer Connections:\n- Connection to public sewer must be made at the designated point with an approved fitting.\n- Where no public sewer is available, the building sewer must connect to an approved private disposal system (septic system).\n- No storm water, surface water, groundwater, or roof drainage shall be connected to a sanitary sewer unless the system is designed as a combined sewer.\n\nStorm Water Drainage:\n- Storm drainage must be separate from the sanitary sewer system unless a combined system is approved.\n- Storm drains must be sized based on the maximum rainfall rate for the area and the horizontal projected roof area.\n- Minimum pipe size for storm drains: 3 inches.\n- Roof drains must be installed at low points of the roof and must be of approved type with strainers.\n\nStorm Drain Sizing (based on rainfall rate, typically 4 inches per hour in Kentucky):\n- 3-inch pipe: approximately 1,600 square feet of roof area\n- 4-inch pipe: approximately 3,600 square feet of roof area\n- 5-inch pipe: approximately 6,200 square feet of roof area\n- 6-inch pipe: approximately 10,200 square feet of roof area\n- 8-inch pipe: approximately 22,000 square feet of roof area\n\nCombined Sewers:\n- Where approved, a combined sewer carries both sanitary waste and storm water.\n- Combined sewer must be sized for the total combined load.\n- All requirements for both sanitary and storm drainage apply.\n\nCleanouts for Building Sewers:\n- Required at the upper terminal (within 5 feet of the building).\n- Required at each change of direction greater than 45 degrees.\n- Required at intervals not exceeding 100 feet.\n- Must be brought to grade level or otherwise made accessible.\n\nSump Pumps and Ejectors:\n- Where gravity drainage is not possible, an approved sewage ejector or sump pump is required.\n- Sewage ejectors must discharge into an approved gravity drainage system.\n- Sump pits must be of approved construction with a sealed, gas-tight cover.\n- Storm water sump pumps must discharge separately from sanitary ejectors.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 5,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-150',
    section_number: '150',
    title: 'Inspections and Tests',
    summary:
      'This section establishes the procedures and requirements for testing and inspecting plumbing systems before they are approved for use. All new plumbing work, additions, and alterations must pass the required tests and inspections before being concealed or placed into service. The plumbing inspector has the authority to require any portion of the work to be exposed for inspection and to order corrections of any violations found.\n\nThe two primary testing methods are the water test and the air test. For the water test, the entire drainage and vent system (or the section being tested) must be filled with water to the highest point and must hold for a minimum of 15 minutes without any drop in the water level. For the air test, the system must be pressurized to 5 PSI and must hold for 15 minutes without any loss of pressure. The air test is typically used in cold weather or where water testing is impractical. All tests must be performed in the presence of the plumbing inspector or as directed.\n\nThe water supply system must also be tested. Water piping must be subjected to a pressure test at the working pressure of the system or a minimum of 80 PSI, whichever is greater, for a minimum of 15 minutes. All joints and connections must be visually inspected during the test for leaks. Final inspection includes verifying that all fixtures are properly installed, trapped, vented, and connected, and that the system operates correctly.',
    full_text:
      'INSPECTIONS AND TESTS (815 KAR 20:150)\n\nGeneral Requirements:\n- All plumbing work must be inspected and tested before being concealed or put into use.\n- The plumbing contractor must notify the inspector when work is ready for testing.\n- No portion of a plumbing system shall be covered or concealed until it has been inspected and approved.\n- The inspector has the authority to require the uncovering of any plumbing work for examination.\n\nTypes of Inspections:\n1. Underground (rough-in below grade): Inspection of all underground piping before backfill.\n2. Rough-in (above grade): Inspection of all drainage, waste, vent, and water piping before walls and ceilings are closed.\n3. Final: Inspection after all fixtures are installed and the system is complete.\n4. Special or re-inspection: As required by the inspector.\n\nDrainage and Vent System Tests:\n\nWater Test:\n- The entire system (or section under test) must be filled with water to the highest opening.\n- All openings must be plugged or capped except the highest opening.\n- The water must stand for a minimum of 15 minutes.\n- There must be no drop in the water level during the test period.\n- The test may be applied to the entire system at once or in sections.\n\nAir Test:\n- The system must be pressurized to a gauge pressure of 5 PSI (pounds per square inch).\n- The pressure must hold for a minimum of 15 minutes.\n- There must be no loss of pressure during the test period.\n- Air test is an alternative to the water test, often used in cold weather conditions.\n\nSmoke Test:\n- May be used for existing systems to detect leaks and defects.\n- Not typically used for new construction testing.\n\nWater Supply System Test:\n- Water piping must be tested at the working pressure or 80 PSI minimum, whichever is greater.\n- Duration: minimum 15 minutes.\n- All joints and connections must be visually examined for leaks during the test.\n- No drop in pressure is permitted.\n\nFinal Inspection Requirements:\n- All fixtures must be properly installed, secured, and operational.\n- All fixtures must be properly trapped and vented.\n- Hot and cold water must be available at all required fixtures.\n- All valves must operate properly.\n- Fixture clearances must meet code requirements.\n- All required cleanouts must be accessible.\n- The system must be free of cross-connections.\n\nDocumentation:\n- The inspector shall maintain records of all inspections and tests.\n- An approval or certificate of compliance shall be issued when the system passes all required tests.\n- Deficiencies must be corrected and the system re-inspected before approval.\n\nRe-Inspection:\n- If work fails an inspection, the plumber must correct the deficiencies.\n- The corrected work must be re-inspected.\n- Additional re-inspection fees may apply.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 6,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-170',
    section_number: '170',
    title: 'Manufactured Home and Mobile Home Community Waste Systems',
    summary:
      'This section covers the plumbing requirements specific to manufactured homes (mobile homes) and manufactured home communities. It establishes the standards for waste disposal systems, water supply connections, and drainage systems within manufactured home parks and for individual manufactured home installations throughout Kentucky.\n\nManufactured homes must be connected to approved water supply and sewage disposal systems. Each home must have an individual water shutoff valve and an individual sewer connection with a proper watertight fitting. The waste system within the community must be designed and sized to handle the total fixture load from all homes connected to it. Where a community connects to a public sewer, the system must comply with all applicable building sewer requirements.\n\nEach manufactured home site must have a drain inlet that is properly trapped and vented. The connection between the manufactured home drain outlet and the site drain inlet must be made with approved materials and fittings designed for this purpose. The connections must be accessible for inspection and maintenance. Where a private sewage disposal system (septic system) is used, it must comply with all state and local health department regulations.',
    full_text:
      'MANUFACTURED HOME AND MOBILE HOME COMMUNITY WASTE SYSTEMS (815 KAR 20:170)\n\nGeneral Requirements:\n- All plumbing in manufactured home communities must comply with the Kentucky State Plumbing Code.\n- Each manufactured home site must have an approved water supply connection and an approved sewer connection.\n- A licensed plumber must perform all plumbing work on the community distribution and collection systems.\n\nWater Supply:\n- Each site must have an individual water service connection with a shutoff valve.\n- Minimum water service pipe to each site: 3/4 inch.\n- The community water distribution system must be sized to provide adequate flow and pressure to all sites under peak demand.\n- Backflow prevention must be provided at each site connection.\n- The water supply must meet all applicable drinking water standards.\n\nSewer Connections:\n- Each manufactured home site must have a sewer inlet connection.\n- Minimum sewer connection size: 4 inches.\n- The connection must be watertight and made with approved fittings.\n- A trap is required at or near the site sewer inlet.\n- The sewer connection must be capped when a site is unoccupied.\n\nCommunity Sewer System:\n- The community sewer collection system must be designed to handle the total DFU load from all occupied sites.\n- Minimum slope requirements for community sewer mains are the same as for building sewers (1/8 inch per foot for 4-inch and larger).\n- Cleanouts are required at intervals not exceeding 100 feet and at all changes of direction greater than 45 degrees.\n- Manholes are required at intervals not exceeding 400 feet on sewer mains 6 inches and larger.\n\nManufactured Home Connections:\n- The drain outlet of the manufactured home must connect to the site sewer inlet with approved fittings.\n- Flexible connections, where used, must be of approved type and material.\n- All connections must be accessible for inspection.\n- The connection point must be protected from physical damage and frost.\n\nPrivate Disposal Systems:\n- Where public sewer is not available, the community may use an approved private disposal system (septic system or treatment plant).\n- Private systems must comply with Kentucky Division of Water and local health department requirements.\n- Septic tanks and drain fields must be sized for the total anticipated load from the community.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 7,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-180',
    section_number: '180',
    title: 'Special Connections',
    summary:
      'This section addresses plumbing connections that require special treatment due to the nature of the waste being discharged or the risk of contamination. Indirect waste connections, acid waste systems, dilution pits, grease interceptors, oil separators, and similar special waste handling systems are covered here. These systems protect the public sewer and the plumbing system from damage, contamination, and hazardous materials.\n\nIndirect waste connections are required for equipment and fixtures where a direct connection to the drainage system could create a health hazard through backflow or contamination. This includes food preparation equipment (commercial dishwashers, ice machines, food processing equipment), sterilizers, refrigeration condensate drains, and similar fixtures. An indirect waste pipe must discharge into the drainage system through an air gap or air break into a properly trapped and vented floor sink, floor drain, or other approved receptor.\n\nGrease interceptors are required for commercial kitchens and food service establishments to prevent fats, oils, and grease from entering the public sewer system. The interceptor must be properly sized based on the flow rate and must be accessible for cleaning and maintenance. Acid waste systems are required in laboratories, industrial facilities, and other locations where corrosive chemicals are discharged. Acid-resistant pipe and fittings must be used, and a dilution pit or neutralizing tank may be required before the waste enters the sanitary sewer.',
    full_text:
      'SPECIAL CONNECTIONS (815 KAR 20:180)\n\nIndirect Waste Connections:\n- Required for fixtures and equipment where a direct connection would create a cross-connection hazard.\n- Indirect waste pipes must discharge through an air gap into a trapped and vented receptor.\n- Minimum air gap for indirect waste: 1 inch or twice the diameter of the indirect waste pipe, whichever is greater.\n- Equipment requiring indirect waste connections includes:\n  - Commercial food preparation equipment\n  - Commercial dishwashers\n  - Ice machines\n  - Refrigerator condensate drains\n  - Sterilizers and autoclaves\n  - Water softener discharge\n  - Air conditioning condensate drains\n  - Swimming pool and spa equipment\n  - Commercial laundry equipment\n\nFloor Sinks and Receptors:\n- Must be of approved materials with smooth, impervious surfaces.\n- Must be properly trapped and vented.\n- Must be of adequate size to receive the discharge without overflow.\n- Must be accessible for cleaning.\n\nGrease Interceptors:\n- Required for all commercial kitchens, restaurants, and food service establishments.\n- Must be installed in the waste line downstream of all fixtures that produce grease-laden waste.\n- Garbage disposals (food waste grinders) shall NOT discharge through the grease interceptor.\n- Dishwashers shall NOT discharge through the grease interceptor (hot water emulsifies grease and reduces interceptor effectiveness).\n- Grease interceptors must be properly sized based on flow rate and capacity.\n- Must be accessible for regular cleaning and maintenance.\n- Must have a flow control device.\n- Exterior (in-ground) grease interceptors are typically required for commercial food service.\n\nOil and Sand Interceptors:\n- Required for repair garages, gasoline stations, car washes, and similar establishments.\n- Must be designed to prevent oil, grease, sand, and other substances from entering the sewer.\n- Must be properly sized and accessible for cleaning.\n\nAcid Waste Systems:\n- Required in laboratories, industrial facilities, and other locations discharging corrosive waste.\n- Acid waste piping must be of acid-resistant material (borosilicate glass, polypropylene, PVDF, or other approved material).\n- Acid waste must be neutralized before entering the sanitary sewer.\n- A dilution pit or neutralizing basin is required.\n- The dilution pit must contain neutralizing media (typically limestone chips).\n- Dilution pits must be accessible for inspection and maintenance of neutralizing media.\n\nDilution Pits:\n- Used to neutralize acid waste before discharge to the sewer.\n- Must be constructed of acid-resistant materials.\n- Must contain neutralizing material (limestone chips or marble chips).\n- Must be sized based on the volume of acid waste discharge.\n- Must have a cleanout and must be accessible.\n\nSpecial Waste Systems:\n- Radioactive waste: Must comply with Nuclear Regulatory Commission requirements.\n- Infectious waste: Must comply with health department requirements.\n- Chemical waste: Must be pretreated or hauled to an approved disposal facility.\n- No hazardous waste shall be discharged directly into a public sewer without pretreatment.\n\nCondensate Waste:\n- Air conditioning and refrigeration condensate must be drained by indirect waste to an approved receptor.\n- Condensate drains must be trapped if connected to the drainage system.\n- Condensate may discharge to the outdoors if approved by the authority having jurisdiction.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 8,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-191',
    section_number: '191',
    title: 'Minimum Fixture Requirements',
    summary:
      'This section establishes the minimum number of plumbing fixtures required in buildings based on the occupancy type and the number of occupants. The fixture counts ensure adequate sanitary facilities are available for the building users and are typically calculated using tables that specify the ratio of fixtures to occupants for each occupancy classification. This section is frequently referenced in the exam.\n\nThe occupancy classifications include Assembly (theaters, churches, stadiums), Business (offices, banks, professional buildings), Educational (schools), Factory/Industrial, Institutional (hospitals, prisons), Mercantile (retail stores), Residential, and Storage. For each classification, the code specifies the minimum number of water closets, urinals (where applicable), lavatories, drinking fountains, and service sinks per number of occupants.\n\nWhen calculating the number of required fixtures, the total occupant load is divided equally between male and female unless the actual distribution is known. In assembly occupancies, the ratio of water closets for women is typically greater than for men. Employee facilities must be provided separately from public facilities in mercantile and business occupancies when the number of employees exceeds a threshold. Accessible (ADA-compliant) fixtures must be included as part of the required minimum.',
    full_text:
      'MINIMUM FIXTURE REQUIREMENTS (815 KAR 20:191)\n\nGeneral Rules:\n- The minimum number of fixtures is based on occupancy type and occupant load.\n- When calculating occupant load, assume equal distribution between male and female unless specific data is available.\n- Fixtures must be distributed among the floors of a multi-story building.\n- Employee facilities may be required separately from public facilities.\n- Accessible fixtures must be provided in compliance with ADA and Kentucky accessibility requirements.\n\nMinimum Fixture Counts by Occupancy Type:\n\nAssembly (A) - Theaters, Auditoriums, Convention Halls:\n- Water closets: 1 per 125 males, 1 per 65 females\n- Urinals: may substitute for up to 50% of required male water closets\n- Lavatories: 1 per 200 persons\n- Drinking fountains: 1 per 500 persons\n- Service sink: 1 per floor minimum\n\nBusiness (B) - Offices, Professional Buildings:\n- Water closets: 1 per 25 for the first 50, 1 per 50 for the remainder (each sex)\n- Lavatories: 1 per 40 persons\n- Drinking fountains: 1 per 100 persons\n- Service sink: 1 per floor minimum\n\nEducational (E) - Schools:\n- Water closets: 1 per 30 males, 1 per 25 females\n- Urinals: 1 per 30 males\n- Lavatories: 1 per 35 persons\n- Drinking fountains: 1 per 100 persons\n- Service sink: 1 per floor minimum\n\nFactory/Industrial (F):\n- Water closets: 1 per 30 for the first 100, 1 per 50 for the remainder (each sex)\n- Lavatories: 1 per 40 persons (individual or circular wash fountains)\n- Drinking fountains: 1 per 100 persons\n- Service sink: 1 per floor minimum\n- Showers may be required based on the nature of the work.\n\nInstitutional (I) - Hospitals, Nursing Homes:\n- Patient rooms: 1 water closet, 1 lavatory per room or per 2 beds\n- Nurse stations: appropriate fixtures as required\n- Waiting rooms: follow Assembly occupancy requirements\n- Drinking fountains: 1 per 100 persons (or per floor)\n- Service sink: 1 per floor minimum\n\nMercantile (M) - Retail Stores:\n- For customers (where required): 1 water closet per 500 persons (each sex)\n- Employee facilities: 1 water closet per 25 for the first 50, 1 per 50 for the remainder\n- Lavatories: 1 per 40 employees\n- Drinking fountains: 1 per 100 persons\n- Service sink: 1 per floor minimum\n\nResidential (R):\n- Each dwelling unit: minimum 1 water closet, 1 lavatory, 1 bathtub or shower, 1 kitchen sink.\n- Hotels/Motels: each guest room must have 1 water closet, 1 lavatory, 1 bathtub or shower.\n- Dormitories: 1 water closet per 10, 1 lavatory per 10, 1 shower per 8.\n\nStorage (S):\n- Water closets: 1 per 50 for the first 100, 1 per 100 for the remainder (each sex)\n- Lavatories: 1 per 80 persons\n- Drinking fountains: 1 per 100 persons\n- Service sink: 1 per building minimum\n\nAdditional Requirements:\n- Where more than one restroom is required, they must be distributed to be accessible without traveling more than one floor.\n- Signs must clearly indicate the location of public restrooms.\n- All public restrooms must be maintained in a sanitary condition.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 9,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-195',
    section_number: '195',
    title: 'Medical Gas Piping Installations',
    summary:
      'This section addresses the installation requirements for medical gas and vacuum systems in healthcare facilities throughout Kentucky. Medical gas systems supply oxygen, nitrous oxide, medical air, nitrogen, and carbon dioxide to patient care areas, operating rooms, and other clinical spaces. These systems are life-safety critical and must be installed by qualified personnel with specific training and certification in medical gas piping.\n\nAll medical gas piping installations must comply with NFPA 99 (Health Care Facilities Code) and must be performed by installers who hold a current ASSE 6010 certification (or equivalent as approved). The piping must be copper tube of medical gas grade, cleaned for oxygen service, and brazed with a nitrogen purge to prevent oxidation inside the pipe. All joints must be brazed (not soldered), and the brazing alloy must be a BCuP series (silver-bearing) alloy meeting AWS A5.8 specifications.\n\nAfter installation, the entire medical gas system must undergo a rigorous series of tests including initial pressure testing, cross-connection testing, purge testing, and final verification by a qualified verifier (ASSE 6030 certified). The system cannot be placed into service until all tests are passed and documented. Source equipment, alarm systems, and zone valve boxes must also be inspected and verified. Proper labeling and color coding of all medical gas piping is required to prevent dangerous cross-connections.',
    full_text:
      'MEDICAL GAS PIPING INSTALLATIONS (815 KAR 20:195)\n\nGeneral Requirements:\n- All medical gas and vacuum piping systems must comply with NFPA 99 (Health Care Facilities Code).\n- Medical gas systems include: oxygen, nitrous oxide, medical air, nitrogen, carbon dioxide, and medical vacuum.\n- Only qualified, certified installers may perform medical gas piping work.\n- Installer qualification: ASSE 6010 Medical Gas Systems Installer certification (or equivalent approved certification).\n\nPiping Materials:\n- Pipe: Copper tube, Type K or Type L, cleaned for oxygen service per CGA G-4.1.\n- Fittings: Wrought copper (solder fittings are NOT permitted; brazing only).\n- Brazing alloy: BCuP series (silver-bearing) brazing alloy per AWS A5.8.\n- All brazing must be performed with a continuous nitrogen purge inside the pipe to prevent oxide formation.\n- No flux is permitted inside medical gas piping.\n\nInstallation Requirements:\n- Piping must be clearly labeled at intervals of no more than 20 feet and at least once in every room.\n- Labels must identify the gas type and include directional flow arrows.\n- Color coding must conform to NFPA 99 (Oxygen: green; Nitrous Oxide: blue; Medical Air: yellow; Nitrogen: black; Vacuum: white; Carbon Dioxide: gray).\n- Piping must be supported at intervals per the copper tube support schedule.\n- Medical gas piping must not be installed in the same conduit, chase, or channel with electrical wiring.\n- Shutoff valves (zone valves, riser valves, service valves) must be accessible and properly labeled.\n- Zone valve boxes are required to isolate sections of the system for maintenance without shutting down the entire system.\n\nSource Equipment:\n- Bulk oxygen systems, cylinder manifolds, medical air compressors, and vacuum pumps must be installed per NFPA 99.\n- Medical air compressors must have intake located outdoors, away from exhaust vents, and at a minimum height above grade.\n- Vacuum pump exhaust must be discharged outdoors.\n\nAlarm Systems:\n- Master alarm panels are required in the maintenance office or other continuously monitored location.\n- Area alarm panels are required at nurse stations or other appropriate locations.\n- Alarms must monitor pressure in all medical gas systems and provide both visual and audible signals.\n\nTesting and Verification:\n- All medical gas piping must undergo the following tests after installation:\n  1. Initial pressure test: 150 PSI for 24 hours (standing pressure test, no drop permitted).\n  2. Cross-connection test: each outlet must be verified to deliver the correct gas.\n  3. Purge test: system must be purged with the appropriate gas until purity levels meet requirements.\n  4. Final verification: performed by an ASSE 6030 certified verifier.\n- The system must NOT be placed into service until all tests are passed and documentation is complete.\n- Test records must be maintained permanently by the facility.\n\nModifications and Repairs:\n- Any modification or addition to an existing medical gas system must be performed by certified installers.\n- Modified sections must undergo the same testing and verification requirements as new installations.\n- The existing system must be protected from contamination during any modification work.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 10,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-krs318',
    section_number: 'KRS318',
    title: 'Kentucky Plumbing Law (KRS Chapter 318)',
    summary:
      'KRS Chapter 318 is the Kentucky state law that governs the plumbing profession, establishing the licensing framework and legal requirements for anyone performing plumbing work in the Commonwealth. This chapter creates the Kentucky Department of Housing, Buildings and Construction as the regulatory authority and defines the classifications of plumbing licenses: Master Plumber, Journeyman Plumber, and Apprentice Plumber. Understanding these definitions and the requirements for each license level is essential for the exam.\n\nA Master Plumber is defined as a person who has the necessary qualifications, training, experience, and technical knowledge to properly plan, superintend, and install plumbing in accordance with the state plumbing code and applicable laws. A Journeyman Plumber is a person who has the necessary qualifications and experience to install plumbing under the supervision of a Master Plumber. An Apprentice Plumber is a person who is learning the plumbing trade under the direct supervision of a Journeyman or Master Plumber. Only a Master Plumber may obtain plumbing permits and contract for plumbing work.\n\nThe law establishes grounds for license revocation, suspension, or denial, including fraud, incompetence, violation of the plumbing code, allowing an unlicensed person to work unsupervised, and conviction of a felony. Penalties for performing plumbing work without a license include fines and potential criminal charges. The law also provides for an appeals process and defines the rights and responsibilities of all parties involved in the plumbing licensing system.',
    full_text:
      'KENTUCKY PLUMBING LAW (KRS CHAPTER 318)\n\nLicensing Authority:\n- The Kentucky Department of Housing, Buildings and Construction administers the plumbing licensing program.\n- The Plumbing Section oversees examinations, licensing, inspections, and enforcement.\n\nLicense Classifications:\n\nMaster Plumber:\n- Definition: A person who has the necessary qualifications, training, experience, and technical knowledge to properly plan, superintend, and install plumbing and who has passed the state Master Plumber examination.\n- A Master Plumber may contract for and obtain permits for plumbing work.\n- A Master Plumber is responsible for the supervision of Journeymen and Apprentices working under their license.\n- Must have completed the required years of experience as a Journeyman (typically 4 years as a Journeyman, or equivalent combination of education and experience).\n- Must pass the Kentucky Master Plumber examination.\n\nJourneyman Plumber:\n- Definition: A person who has the necessary qualifications and experience to install plumbing under the direction and supervision of a Master Plumber.\n- A Journeyman may perform plumbing work but may NOT contract for or obtain permits.\n- Must have completed the required apprenticeship period or equivalent experience.\n- Must pass the Kentucky Journeyman Plumber examination.\n- Must work under the supervision of a Master Plumber.\n\nApprenticePlumber:\n- Definition: A person who is learning the plumbing trade by working under the direct supervision of a Journeyman Plumber or Master Plumber.\n- Must be registered with the state.\n- Must work under direct supervision at all times.\n- The ratio of apprentices to Journeymen/Masters is regulated.\n- Must complete the required term of apprenticeship before being eligible for the Journeyman examination.\n\nLicense Requirements:\n- All persons performing plumbing work in Kentucky must hold a valid plumbing license or be a registered apprentice working under supervision.\n- Licenses must be renewed periodically (annually or biennially as required).\n- Continuing education may be required for license renewal.\n- A homeowner may perform plumbing work on their own single-family residence (homeowner exemption), but the work must still comply with the plumbing code and must be inspected.\n\nGrounds for License Revocation, Suspension, or Denial:\n- Fraud or misrepresentation in obtaining a license.\n- Gross incompetence or negligence in performing plumbing work.\n- Violation of the Kentucky State Plumbing Code.\n- Allowing an unlicensed or unregistered person to perform plumbing work without proper supervision.\n- Failure to obtain required permits before performing work.\n- Conviction of a felony.\n- Performing work outside the scope of the license held.\n- Repeated failure to correct code violations.\n- Aiding and abetting an unlicensed person in performing plumbing work.\n\nPenalties:\n- Performing plumbing work without a license: subject to fines and criminal penalties.\n- Each day of violation may constitute a separate offense.\n- Fines may range from $100 to $1,000 or more per violation.\n- Criminal penalties may include misdemeanor charges.\n- The department may seek injunctive relief to stop unlicensed work.\n\nAppeals Process:\n- Any person aggrieved by a decision of the department may appeal.\n- Appeals must be filed within the time period specified by law.\n- A hearing is provided before an administrative law judge.\n- Further appeal may be made to the circuit court.\n\nPermits:\n- A plumbing permit is required before commencing plumbing work (except minor repairs).\n- Only a licensed Master Plumber may obtain a plumbing permit.\n- The permit must be posted at the job site.\n- Work must be inspected and approved before being concealed or placed into use.\n\nExemptions:\n- Homeowner performing plumbing work on their own single-family residence (must still comply with code and obtain permits/inspections).\n- Minor repairs and maintenance (replacing faucet washers, clearing stoppages) do not require a permit.\n- Utility companies performing work on their own systems up to the point of delivery.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 11,
  },
  {
    id: 'cs-815kar2',
    section_number: '815KAR2',
    title: 'Administrative Regulations (815 KAR Chapter 2)',
    summary:
      'This section covers the administrative regulations that govern the procedural aspects of the plumbing program in Kentucky, codified under 815 KAR Chapter 2. These regulations supplement the plumbing law (KRS 318) by establishing detailed procedures for permits, fees, examinations, inspections, and administrative appeals. Understanding these administrative requirements is important for both the licensing exam and daily practice as a licensed plumber.\n\nThe regulations establish the permit application process, including the information required on the permit application, the fee schedule for permits and inspections, and the timeline for completing work under a permit. Permits are generally valid for a specified period (typically 12 months) and must be renewed if work is not completed within that time. The fee structure is based on the number and type of fixtures being installed, with additional fees for special inspections, re-inspections, and plan reviews.\n\nThe administrative regulations also govern the examination process for plumbing licenses, including eligibility requirements, examination content and format, passing scores, and procedures for re-examination. They establish the continuing education requirements for license renewal and set forth the administrative hearing process for contested cases, including license revocation proceedings. The regulations also address reciprocity with other states and the process for obtaining a Kentucky license based on credentials from another jurisdiction.',
    full_text:
      'ADMINISTRATIVE REGULATIONS (815 KAR CHAPTER 2)\n\nPermit Requirements:\n- A plumbing permit must be obtained before starting any plumbing work (except minor repairs that do not involve altering the drainage, waste, vent, or water distribution systems).\n- The permit application must include:\n  - Name and license number of the Master Plumber.\n  - Location and description of the work.\n  - Number and type of fixtures to be installed.\n  - Estimated cost of the plumbing work (where required).\n- The permit must be posted at the job site in a visible location.\n- Work must commence within a reasonable time after the permit is issued.\n- Permits are valid for a specified period (typically 12 months from date of issue).\n- Expired permits must be renewed before work can continue.\n\nFee Schedule:\n- Permit fees are based on the number and type of fixtures.\n- Typical fee components include:\n  - Base permit fee.\n  - Per-fixture fee for each plumbing fixture installed.\n  - Additional fees for special inspections or plan reviews.\n  - Re-inspection fees when work fails an inspection.\n- Fee amounts are established by regulation and may be updated periodically.\n\nInspection Procedures:\n- The plumber must request inspections at the required stages (underground, rough-in, final).\n- Adequate notice must be given to the inspector (typically 24-48 hours).\n- The work must be accessible and ready for inspection at the scheduled time.\n- If the work is not ready or the plumber is not present, a re-inspection fee may be charged.\n- The inspector will provide written results of each inspection.\n\nExamination Requirements:\n- Journeyman Plumber Examination:\n  - Eligibility: completion of required apprenticeship or equivalent experience.\n  - Content: Kentucky State Plumbing Code, practical plumbing knowledge, mathematics.\n  - Format: written examination (multiple choice and/or practical).\n  - Passing score: as established by the department (typically 70% or higher).\n\n- Master Plumber Examination:\n  - Eligibility: valid Journeyman license plus required years of Journeyman experience.\n  - Content: Kentucky State Plumbing Code, plumbing design and layout, business practices, supervision responsibilities.\n  - Format: written examination.\n  - Passing score: as established by the department (typically 70% or higher).\n\n- Re-examination: candidates who fail may retake the examination after a waiting period.\n\nContinuing Education:\n- Licensed plumbers may be required to complete continuing education hours for license renewal.\n- Approved courses must relate to plumbing code updates, safety, technology, or other relevant topics.\n- Records of continuing education must be maintained by the licensee.\n\nAdministrative Hearings:\n- Contested cases (license denial, revocation, suspension) are heard by an administrative hearing officer.\n- The licensee has the right to notice, representation, and a fair hearing.\n- The burden of proof is on the department to demonstrate grounds for adverse action.\n- Hearing decisions may be appealed to the circuit court.\n\nReciprocity:\n- Kentucky may grant a license to an applicant who holds a valid plumbing license from another state.\n- The applicant must demonstrate that the other state\'s requirements are substantially equivalent to Kentucky\'s.\n- The department may require the applicant to pass the Kentucky examination on state-specific code provisions.\n- Reciprocity is granted at the discretion of the department.\n\nPlan Review:\n- Plans for commercial and institutional plumbing systems may require review and approval before a permit is issued.\n- Plans must be prepared by a licensed professional (engineer or Master Plumber) where required.\n- Plan review fees are separate from permit fees.\n- Approved plans must be kept at the job site during construction.',
    parent_section: null,
    sort_order: 12,
  },
];
